WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.010 --> 00:00:08.640
In 1670 John Ray coined the phrase ‘The
early bird catches the worm’, before him

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:14.340
‘It is well to be up before daybreak, for
such habits contribute to health, wealth,

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:15.910
and wisdom.’ said Aristotle.

00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:21.680
‘Lose an hour in the morning, and you will
be all day hunting for it.’

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:22.890
proclaimed Richard Whately.

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:27.699
It is drilled into our heads throughout our
entire lives that it’s better for us to

00:00:27.699 --> 00:00:30.869
get to bed early and wake up at the crack
of dawn.

00:00:30.869 --> 00:00:36.329
In doing so we can, supposedly, get more done
and live richer, happier lives.

00:00:36.329 --> 00:00:39.149
So, when do you like to go to bed?

00:00:39.149 --> 00:00:45.620
30% of the population are ‘Night Owls’,
they prefer to go to bed late and wake up

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:47.739
late, sometime in the afternoon.

00:00:47.739 --> 00:00:54.170
40% of people are ‘Morning Larks’ they
are more than happy to wake up at the crack

00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:57.379
of dawn but go to bed early.

00:00:57.379 --> 00:01:02.949
And the remaining 30% of people are somewhere
in between but usually have a slight leaning

00:01:02.949 --> 00:01:06.570
towards preferring either evening or morning.

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:09.230
This is referred to by scientists as your
chronotype.

00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:12.690
So, what’s your chronotype?

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:17.610
The issue that night owls have is that they
are socially shunned.

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:20.800
They are called lazy and immature.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:26.400
People often wrongly assume that a night owl’s
inability to wake up early is by choice and

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:32.880
they are berated for this, but the truth is,
such people may not be able to help their

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:38.390
late rising and forcing night owls to wake
up early can actually have disastrous health

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:39.810
consequences.

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:45.120
As far as scientists have observed, all creatures
on Earth, with lifespans longer than a few

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:51.000
days, are subject to a circadian rhythm, in
Latin ‘circa’ meaning ‘around’ and

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:52.770
‘diēm’ for ‘day’.

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:59.340
It is the natural 24-hour repeating sleep-wake
cycle that determines when we feel drowsy

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:04.640
and it’s time for sleep and it also hints
when our bodies should awaken from slumber.

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:12.269
Controlled by the Suprachiasmatic Nucleus
- a tiny part of your brain inside your hypothalamus.

00:02:12.269 --> 00:02:15.420
The Suprachiasmatic Nucleus is your internal
clock.

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:21.459
The Suprachiasmatic Nucleus is excellent at
keeping time without any external influence.

00:02:21.459 --> 00:02:28.319
In 1962 French geologist Michel Siffre spent
two months deep underground in a cave where

00:02:28.319 --> 00:02:31.569
not a splash of light was able to reach him.

00:02:31.569 --> 00:02:38.459
He found that his circadian rhythm was able
to keep his sleep/wake routine accurate to

00:02:38.459 --> 00:02:44.620
around 24 hours, but it would often overshoot
by up to half an hour.

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:50.330
This revealed that our Suprachiasmatic Nucleus
is great at approximating time, but without

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:56.299
a fixed reference it is unable to maintain
it precisely, to the minute.

00:02:56.299 --> 00:03:01.220
We now know that the Suprachiasmatic Nucleus
synchronises itself and thus our circadian

00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:03.560
rhythm with the sun.

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:10.541
Interestingly, however, if you were to, hypothetically,
live in a permanently dark environment, with

00:03:10.541 --> 00:03:16.370
no sun, such as your mother’s basement,
and perhaps you do, then your circadian rhythm

00:03:16.370 --> 00:03:21.420
would sync itself to any other consistency
that happens on a repeating 24-hour cycle,

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:27.749
such as temperature fluctuations or remarkably,
even your daily morning coffee.

00:03:27.749 --> 00:03:34.459
These time aligning events are called Zeitgebers,
literally 'time giver’ and they are how

00:03:34.459 --> 00:03:39.239
blind people maintain their circadian rhythms.

00:03:39.239 --> 00:03:44.209
There are two important chemicals involved
in sleep, melatonin and adenosine.

00:03:44.209 --> 00:03:49.489
Melatonin, released by the pineal gland, communicates
with our Suprachiasmatic Nucleus, telling

00:03:49.489 --> 00:03:52.230
it when its time for sleepiness to kick in.

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:57.279
It is released in large amounts around our
typical bedtime, or annoyingly when you really

00:03:57.279 --> 00:04:01.249
want to watch just one more episode of that
Netflix series.

00:04:01.249 --> 00:04:05.681
Its release slows down whilst we sleep, eventually
halting production altogether when it is time

00:04:05.681 --> 00:04:07.470
for us to wake up.

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:11.010
Think of Adenosine, however, like sleep pressure.

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:16.350
It is released in increasing quantities throughout
the day and it swells up more in volume in

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:18.930
our bloodstream the longer we are awake.

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:24.340
It slows down our neurons which causes us
to feel drowsy.

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:30.700
After this sleep pressure builds up to extreme
levels, we can no longer fight off sleep.

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:33.671
You know that feeling when you’re sat at
your desk in the early afternoon and your

00:04:33.671 --> 00:04:38.510
head suddenly drops into a micro-slumber and
then your neck snaps it back up, because you

00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:42.370
would hate to be fired from yet another job
for sleeping at your desk.

00:04:42.370 --> 00:04:47.200
This is because the levels of Adenosine in
your body have caused your neurons to slow

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:53.090
down to such a level that you can no longer
physically stay awake.

00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:55.470
A power nap is in order.

00:04:55.470 --> 00:05:00.561
This afternoon drowsiness happens to us all
since Adenosine release spikes during the

00:05:00.561 --> 00:05:03.880
afternoon and then again just before bedtime.

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:07.660
So perhaps siestas are just meant to be after
all.

00:05:07.660 --> 00:05:11.880
Although research has shown that this afternoon
drowsiness only occurs in people who regularly

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:16.570
fail to get sufficient sleep at night time,
so if you have experienced this, you need

00:05:16.570 --> 00:05:17.970
more sleep.

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:23.490
Adenosine and your circadian rhythm are completely
independent but related systems.

00:05:23.490 --> 00:05:28.130
If you have ever pulled an all-nighter, you
will have experienced Adenosine in action

00:05:28.130 --> 00:05:31.580
and how it jostles for control with your circadian
rhythm.

00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:36.360
During the first day Adenosine or ‘sleep
pressure’ builds up in your system as normal

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:41.330
and by that evening your circadian rhythm
decides it's time for sleep and you will feel

00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:42.330
tired.

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:47.960
But if you can make it through this period
of extreme drowsiness and low energy levels

00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:53.750
until the morning then you will experience
a second wind.

00:05:53.750 --> 00:05:55.740
But why?

00:05:55.740 --> 00:06:01.750
Adenosine will never stop being released and
broken down - until you actually sleep.

00:06:01.750 --> 00:06:06.690
By morning the Adenosine levels in your body
will have reached extremely high levels but,

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:12.930
nonetheless, you have a new rush of energy
during this time because your circadian rhythm

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:16.830
doesn’t care that you haven’t actually
slept.

00:06:16.830 --> 00:06:23.810
It keeps marching on relentlessly, up and
down, every 24 hours, along with the cycle

00:06:23.810 --> 00:06:25.110
of the sun.

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:30.560
Your Suprachiasmatic Nucleus is an accurate
but somewhat blind timepiece.

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:36.470
So when morning comes your circadian rhythm
does its usual wake up routine and infuses

00:06:36.470 --> 00:06:40.590
your body with a rush of energy that will
last throughout the morning.

00:06:40.590 --> 00:06:45.460
This overrides the neural slowdown caused
by all that built up Adenosine.

00:06:45.460 --> 00:06:51.240
However, as the afternoon draws in on your
second day without sleep your circadian rhythm

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:55.950
starts to wind down your body as bedtime draws
closer yet again.

00:06:55.950 --> 00:07:02.800
But this time, the Adenosine levels in your
body have built up to unholy levels and are

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:10.890
ready to burst, so when these two independent
systems once again sync up you will experience

00:07:10.890 --> 00:07:17.160
an almighty energy crash and sleep is almost
inevitable.

00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:24.740
Research on DNA samples of 90,000 people collected
by 23andMe revealed that our bias towards

00:07:24.740 --> 00:07:32.190
being a Night Owl or a Morning Lark is roughly
50% due to of our DNA and it can never be

00:07:32.190 --> 00:07:38.160
altered, although it does usually shift slightly
more towards a morning bias as we grow older.

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:48.490
It’s believed the reason the population
is split in their DNA preferences for earlier

00:07:48.490 --> 00:07:54.440
or later bedtimes is that in our hunter-gatherer
days having this divide played a crucial and

00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:55.700
beneficial role.

00:07:55.700 --> 00:08:00.700
It’s hardly surprising that in our prehistoric
days, when we lived in tribes and we had rival

00:08:00.700 --> 00:08:06.080
tribes and deadly predators stalking the forests
and plains of an evening, then having some

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:09.740
members of the tribe awake at all times was
quite handy.

00:08:09.740 --> 00:08:17.100
As long as Bob and Steve were awake from 12
am until 5 am when Dave usually woke up, then

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:18.980
they could keep an eye out for Dave.

00:08:18.980 --> 00:08:25.810
And then, Dave would continue the watch after
Bob and Steve fall asleep at 5 am.

00:08:25.810 --> 00:08:32.740
Evolution determined that hardwiring varying
peaks and troughs of awakeness and sleepiness

00:08:32.740 --> 00:08:37.969
for a portion of the population then it would
ensure that there would always be at least

00:08:37.969 --> 00:08:43.950
one person awake in each tribe to keep watch
because these people would feel naturally

00:08:43.950 --> 00:08:48.420
inclined to still be awake whilst others weren't
and more importantly, they would still be

00:08:48.420 --> 00:08:49.730
alert.

00:08:49.730 --> 00:08:56.029
We can shift our circadian rhythm somewhat,
by up to an hour each night, if we change

00:08:56.029 --> 00:08:57.430
our sleep pattern.

00:08:57.430 --> 00:09:03.810
But we will always have a natural circadian
‘template’, a default time of the day

00:09:03.810 --> 00:09:11.390
when we are biologically predetermined to
fall asleep based on our immutable DNA.

00:09:11.390 --> 00:09:16.070
Scientists have now recently been able to
sequence and observe the unique DNA in each

00:09:16.070 --> 00:09:19.960
of us that causes these variations in our
chronotypes.

00:09:19.960 --> 00:09:25.750
So being a night owl is in your DNA, and whether
or not you are lazy, the fact that you may

00:09:25.750 --> 00:09:34.850
struggle to wake up is not your fault but
is in fact hardwired in your biology.

00:09:34.850 --> 00:09:39.170
The brain of a night owls functions best in
the evening.

00:09:39.170 --> 00:09:43.779
When they wake up their prefrontal cortex,
the front part of the brain that is responsible

00:09:43.779 --> 00:09:48.680
for logic and decision making is still ‘offline’.

00:09:48.680 --> 00:09:56.620
MRI brain scans of night owls have revealed
that this part of the brain, in particular,

00:09:56.620 --> 00:10:02.560
has a warm-up phase, where it will slowly
ramp up into life over the course of the morning

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:07.700
and reach peak efficiency in the late afternoon
or early evening.

00:10:07.700 --> 00:10:13.330
Morning larks are the opposite, their prefrontal
cortex is at its peak at dawn and it loses

00:10:13.330 --> 00:10:16.680
effectiveness as the evening draws in.

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:18.510
This begs an important question.

00:10:18.510 --> 00:10:24.550
If 30% of the population are unable to use
their brains in the morning, are employers

00:10:24.550 --> 00:10:28.670
making a mistake in forcing such people to
come into work at 9 am?

00:10:28.670 --> 00:10:33.670
At a time when these people are unable to
work efficiently and may make more mistakes

00:10:33.670 --> 00:10:34.840
in the workplace.

00:10:34.840 --> 00:10:40.960
Furthermore, asking these people to wake up
and come to work so early can be detrimental

00:10:40.960 --> 00:10:42.640
to their health and cause them to die sooner.

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:47.880
Some people may belligerently proclaim ‘I’ll
sleep when I’m dead’ but those people

00:10:47.880 --> 00:10:53.230
will be dead very soon because tens of reputable
studies on millions of people have been conducted

00:10:53.230 --> 00:10:55.950
on the relationship between sleep and health.

00:10:55.950 --> 00:11:00.370
Overwhelmingly these studies have found that
people who don’t get the required eight

00:11:00.370 --> 00:11:03.900
hours of sleep per night die sooner.

00:11:03.900 --> 00:11:10.420
Adults older than 45 who sleep less than six
hours per night are 200% more likely to have

00:11:10.420 --> 00:11:12.670
a heart attack or stroke.

00:11:12.670 --> 00:11:17.730
And at any age, evidence suggests that if
you sleep for less than six hours per night

00:11:17.730 --> 00:11:25.680
for a period of 14 years then your risk of
heart disease increases by a whopping 500%!

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:33.180
Generally, a lack of sleep has been strongly
linked to an increased risk of cancer, heart

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:36.660
disease, diabetes and dementia.

00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:41.870
So then, it seems that more flexible work
schedules are needed worldwide that are better

00:11:41.870 --> 00:11:45.110
tailored to the sleeping habits of individuals.

00:11:45.110 --> 00:11:50.660
Employers will be rewarded with employees
who work harder, smarter, more efficiently

00:11:50.660 --> 00:11:54.490
and importantly don’t die so damn quickly.

00:11:54.490 --> 00:11:58.580
If you are a night owl and you suspect that
your inability to wake up and function early

00:11:58.580 --> 00:12:04.550
in the morning is holding you back in life
then I encourage you to think again.

00:12:04.550 --> 00:12:09.690
Research conducted at Southampton University
analysed the sleeping habits, ie. chronotype,

00:12:09.690 --> 00:12:14.630
health and wealth of over 1,000 subjects in
England.

00:12:14.630 --> 00:12:20.660
Contrary to what every supposed soothsaying
business guru has told you, night owls were

00:12:20.660 --> 00:12:28.350
found to be, on average, wealthier than morning
larks - those who went to bed later had a

00:12:28.350 --> 00:12:32.910
larger mean income and were more likely to
own a car.

00:12:32.910 --> 00:12:36.740
And a later study also found that night owls
were slightly more intelligent than morning

00:12:36.740 --> 00:12:37.800
larks.

00:12:37.800 --> 00:12:41.730
Interestingly these results were still true
even when the participants took intelligence

00:12:41.730 --> 00:12:48.670
tests again in the morning instead of the
afternoon - night owls still performed better,

00:12:48.670 --> 00:12:53.900
during a time of the day when their neural
functionality is supposed to be suboptimal.

00:12:53.900 --> 00:13:00.000
It’s not all bad for morning chronotypes,
however, In 2012 psychologists at the University

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:04.839
of Toronto studied over 700 people and found
that morning larks are on average happier

00:13:04.839 --> 00:13:07.050
and have a better general mood throughout
the day.

00:13:07.050 --> 00:13:12.420
Morning larks are also more agreeable, more
cooperative and more conscientious.

